{"kind": "journal", "text": "Approach: start with a broad survey of raw primitives, then refine only families that show a smooth plateau rather than an isolated spike, since plateaus survive out of sample and spikes usually do not. Batch 1 showed the panel is dominated by short-horizon reversal (ret 3 to 10 all near SR -1.1, so negated they are +1.1) with weak and inconsistent long-horizon momentum. Batch 2 and 3 showed two normalizations each add roughly 0.5 SR: per-asset time-series z-scoring and division by trailing volatility. Batch 3 surfaced a much stronger primitive, ts z-scored distance above the rolling minimum (an oversold or drawdown state variable), which only works once it is z-scored per asset because its raw cross-sectional level is dominated by asset-specific scale. Batch 5 combined both normalizations for SR above 2. Guiding principle for the final three: pick from the interior of a broad parameter plateau, keep the structures economically interpretable as risk-normalized mean reversion, and diversify across two distinct mechanisms so that one family failing out of sample does not sink all three picks.", "ts": "2026-09-01T06:49:59.568469+00:00"}
