{"kind": "journal", "text": "Final selection logic. The panel is dominated by risk-normalized mean reversion. Three structural findings replicated across independent formulations. First, per-asset time-series z-scoring is essential: raw cross-sectional levels of state variables like distance above the rolling minimum are dominated by asset-specific scale, and z-scoring lifted that signal from SR 0.5 to 1.9. Second, dividing by trailing volatility adds roughly another 0.5 SR and is almost completely insensitive to the window chosen, from std 10 through std 120 the blend scores 3.03 to 3.10, which is a wide plateau rather than a tuned point. Third, a negative-skew overlay is close to orthogonal to the reversal core: alone it earns about 2.0 but blended it lifts the core from 2.6 to above 3.0, and that lift holds across every parameter pairing tested. Confidence that this is not fitting noise comes from replication: the extremum-based minr(60) version and the centered ma(60) version are different primitives measuring the same idea, and both independently exceed SR 2.3, while the horizon response is a smooth hump peaking at 60 days with declines on both sides rather than an isolated spike. Chose the final three from the plateau interior rather than the train maximum of 3.103. Picks one and two are the same two-leg structure evaluated at two different points on the flat denominator plateau, which is a form of parameter averaging. Pick three adds the ma leg as a third mechanism, giving up only 0.09 of train SR in exchange for not depending on any single primitive if minr degrades out of sample. Known caveat: the alpha decays quickly, a three-day delay cuts SR from 2.6 to 1.3, so this is a high-turnover signal and the reported numbers are gross of costs.", "ts": "2026-09-01T07:04:43.606026+00:00"}
{"kind": "report", "top3_chosen": ["add(cs_z(div(neg(ts_z(minr(60), 60)), std(120))), cs_z(div(neg(ts_z(skew(60), 60)), std(20))))", "add(cs_z(div(neg(ts_z(minr(60), 60)), std(60))), cs_z(div(neg(ts_z(skew(60), 60)), std(40))))", "add(add(cs_z(div(neg(ts_z(minr(60), 60)), std(120))), cs_z(div(neg(ts_z(skew(60), 60)), std(20)))), cs_z(div(ts_z(ma(60), 60), std(120))))"], "chosen_sr_train": [3.0738, 3.0505, 2.9855], "rationale": "All three are volatility-normalized mean-reversion blends. Core leg is the per-asset time-series z-score of distance above the rolling 60-day minimum, negated and scaled by inverse trailing volatility, which buys assets unusually depressed relative to their own recent range. Second leg is a negatively signed z-scored 60-day return skew, similarly vol-scaled, which is close to orthogonal and lifts the core from SR 2.6 to above 3.0. Picks one and two are the identical structure evaluated at two different points on a very flat denominator plateau where every combination from std 10 to std 120 scores between 3.03 and 3.10, so this is parameter averaging rather than tuning. Pick three adds a third leg built on the centered moving-average formulation of the same oversold state, sacrificing 0.09 train SR so that no pick depends on a single primitive. Selected from the plateau interior rather than the train maximum of 3.103, and supported by replication across two independent feature formulations and a smooth horizon response peaking at 60 days.", "n_batches_used": 12, "ts": "2026-09-01T07:04:52.032818+00:00"}
